{"causes":["The Renaissance fostered a revival of interest in folk tales and oral traditions, influencing the Brothers Grimm to collect and publish these stories.","The rise of nationalism in 19th century Europe motivated the preservation of cultural heritage through folklore, leading to the compilation of tales like this one.","The impact of the Romantic movement emphasized the value of storytelling and the common people's narratives, paving the way for the Brothers Grimm's work. "],"effects":["The story has been adapted into countless formats, including films, plays, and children's books, expanding its reach and influence.","It contributed to the establishment of the genre of fairy tales as a legitimate form of literature and cultural scholarship.","The moral lessons within the tale have been utilized in educational settings to teach values and critical thinking to children. "],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"brothers-grimm","sourceName":"Brothers Grimm","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"the-wolf-and-the-seven-young-goats","targetName":"The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats","context":"The Brothers Grimm collected and published the tale in their 1812 anthology, "Grimm's Fairy Tales.""},{"sourceSlug":"grimm-fairy-tales","sourceName":"Grimm's Fairy Tales","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-wolf-and-the-seven-young-goats","targetName":"The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats","context":"The collection of Grimm's Fairy Tales provided a framework that shaped the narrative structure and moral lessons found in "The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats.""},{"sourceSlug":"germany","sourceName":"Germany","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"the-wolf-and-the-seven-young-goats","targetName":"The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats","context":"The tale originates from Germany, reflecting the folklore traditions prevalent in the region during the early 19th century."},{"sourceSlug":"fairy-tale-adaptations","sourceName":"Fairy Tale Adaptations","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"the-wolf-and-the-seven-young-goats","targetName":"The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats","context":"Various adaptations have transmitted the themes of caution and morality inherent in the tale to contemporary audiences."},{"sourceSlug":"cultural-heritage","sourceName":"Cultural Heritage","verb":"FRAMES","targetSlug":"the-wolf-and-the-seven-young-goats","targetName":"The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats","context":"The tale is framed within the context of cultural heritage, emphasizing the importance of folklore in understanding societal values."}],"places":[{"name":"Hanau, Germany","role":"Birthplace of the Brothers Grimm"},{"name":"Berlin, Germany","role":"Publication site of Grimm's Fairy Tales"},{"name":"Bremen, Germany","role":"Cultural hub for fairy tales"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-wolf-and-the-seven-young-goats"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11848","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wolf_and_the_Seven_Young_Goats"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats (German: Der Wolf und die sieben jungen Geißlein) is a German fairy tale recorded by the Brothers Grimm in Kinder- und Hausmärchen ('Children's and Household Tales') as tale KHM 5, in the first edition (1812). The tale narrates the story of a mother goat who goes to fetch food and warns her seven young kids not to open the door to the wolf; the wolf attempts three times to enter, disguising his voice and his paw — on his third attempt (with chalk to whiten his paw) he succeeds in entering while the mother is away and swallows six of the seven kids, while the seventh hides in the clock case; the mother returns, discovers what has happened, finds the wolf sleeping under a tree with his swollen belly, cuts him open with scissors, frees the six kids (still alive), fills the wolf's belly with stones, and the wolf drowns when he tries to drink from a well.

The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats is classified as ATU tale type 123 ('The Wolf and the Kids') and shares its central motif with the Little Red Riding Hood tale type (ATU 333) — the wolf who deceives and swallows the victim whole, and the rescue by cutting open the wolf's belly — making it part of a related complex of wolf tales in European folk tradition that explore the threat of deception and the danger of opening the door to strangers.

The Wolf and the Seven Young Goats is one of the earliest tales in the Grimm collection (KHM 5) and one of the most widely taught in German-speaking countries as an early childhood protective narrative — a story that teaches children about the danger of strangers, deception, and the importance of not opening the door to those who claim to be someone they are not. Its central pedagogical message (do not be deceived by appearances; do not open the door to strangers) is one of the most direct child safety narratives in the fairy tale tradition.","subjects":["German Literature","19th Century","Fairy Tale","Brothers Grimm","Folk Literature","Child Safety","Children's Literature","Wolf Folklore"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The tale has significantly influenced the genre of fairy tales, contributing to the cultural literacy of generations and shaping moral education for children, with its adaptations reaching millions worldwide.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
